      "title": "The Limits of Quiet Resignation from the U.S. Military",
      "summary": "<p>According to CNN reporting, Joint Chiefs Chairman Gen. Dan Caine recently asked his top lawyer: &#8220;How should a military commander respond if they determine they have received an unlawful order?&#8221; The lawyer&#8217;s answer was reportedly unequivocal: &#8220;Request to retire &#8212; and refrain from resigning in protest, which could be seen as a political act, or picking a fight to get fired.&#8221; In other words, when given an unlawful order, the commander should resign quietly.That advice, if given as reported, misapplies the idea of quiet resignation in multiple ways. (And I have grave doubts about the accuracy of this reporting.) Indeed,</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://warontherocks.com/the-limits-of-quiet-resignation-from-the-u-s-military/\">The Limits of Quiet Resignation from the U.S. Military</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://warontherocks.com\">War on the Rocks</a>.</p>",
